116 0.00875135 11.8316 0 10.7503V9.23594C0.0114424 8.15897 0.0804759 7.08338 0.206805 6.01379C0.298966 5.29054 0.572003 4.60222 1.00067 4.01245C1.53043 3.47788 2.24944 3.1735 3.002 3.16522C5.7972 2.96509 10.0067 2.96509 10.0067 2.96509C10.0067 2.96509 14.2028 2.96509 17.0047 3.15188C17.7572 3.16015 18.4762 3.46454 19.006 3.99911ZM7.93863 6.96776V12.5915L13.3422 9.79631L7.93863 6.96776Z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></a><a class="text-inverted me-3" href="https://www.threads.com/@natgeosociety/" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="1rem" height="1rem" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" color="currentColor" data-test-id="ng-icon"><path d="M14.7433 9.26961C14.6571 9.22832 14.5696 9.18858 14.481 9.15053C14.3266 6.30606 12.7723 4.6776 10.1625 4.66094C10.1507 4.66086 10.139 4.66086 10.1271 4.66086C8.56613 4.66086 7.26787 5.32718 6.4688 6.53966L7.90411 7.52425C8.50105 6.61857 9.43788 6.4255 10.1278 6.4255C10.1358 6.4255 10.1438 6.4255 10.1517 6.42557C11.011 6.43105 11.6594 6.6809 12.0791 7.16813C12.3845 7.52284 12.5888 8.01302 12.6899 8.63165C11.9281 8.50216 11.1041 8.46234 10.2233 8.51284C7.74199 8.65577 6.14683 10.1029 6.25396 12.1137C6.30833 13.1337 6.81647 14.0112 7.68473 14.5845C8.41884 15.0691 9.36432 15.306 10.347 15.2524C11.6446 15.1812 12.6626 14.6861 13.3729 13.7808C13.9122 13.0933 14.2534 12.2024 14.404 11.0798C15.0224 11.453 15.4808 11.9442 15.7339 12.5346C16.1643 13.5382 16.1894 15.1875 14.8437 16.5321C13.6646 17.71 12.2473 18.2196 10.1054 18.2353C7.72944 18.2177 5.93252 17.4557 4.76422 15.9705C3.6702 14.5798 3.10481 12.571 3.08371 10C3.10481 7.42894 3.6702 5.42017 4.76422 4.02945C5.93252 2.54426 7.72941 1.78229 10.1054 1.76464C12.4986 1.78243 14.3268 2.54806 15.5398 4.04042C16.1346 4.77225 16.5831 5.69258 16.8787 6.76566L18.5607 6.3169C18.2023 4.99606 17.6385 3.85789 16.8712 2.91396C15.3161 1.0007 13.0417 0.0203279 10.1112 0H10.0995C7.175 0.0202573 4.9261 1.00435 3.41529 2.92493C2.07087 4.634 1.37738 7.01205 1.35408 9.99297L1.354 10L1.35408 10.007C1.37738 12.9879 2.07087 15.366 3.41529 17.0751C4.9261 18.9956 7.175 19.9798 10.0995 20H10.1112C12.7113 19.982 14.544 19.3013 16.0538 17.7928C18.0291 15.8194 17.9696 13.3457 17.3186 11.8272C16.8515 10.7382 15.961 9.85377 14.7433 9.26961ZM10.2541 13.4903C9.16656 13.5516 8.03675 13.0634 7.98105 12.0179C7.93975 11.2427 8.53273 10.3777 10.3208 10.2747C10.5256 10.2629 10.7265 10.2571 10.9239 10.2571C11.5734 10.2571 12.181 10.3202 12.7334 10.4409C12.5273 13.0141 11.3188 13.4319 10.2541 13.4903Z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg></a></div></div><div data-test-id="col" class="col-xl-9 col-lg-8 col-md-7 col-12"><p class="text-color-gray-500 mb-0 no-limit-paragraphs">National Geographic Society is a 501 (c)(3) organization. © 1996 -<!-- --> <!-- -->2026<!-- --> National Geographic Society. All rights reserved.</p><span><a class="text-color-gray-500" href="https://www.nationalgeographic.org/code-of-ethics/" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">Code of Ethics</a><span class="mx-1 text-color-gray-500">|</span></span><span><a class="text-color-gray-500" href="https://www.nationalgeographic.org/state-disclosures/" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">State Disclosures</a><span class="mx-1 text-color-gray-500">|</span></span><span><a class="text-color-gray-500" href="https://www.nationalgeographic.org/terms-of-service/" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">Terms of Service</a><span class="mx-1 text-color-gray-500">|</span></span><span><a class="text-color-gray-500" href="https://www.nationalgeographic.org/privacy-notice/" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">Privacy Notice</a><span class="mx-1 text-color-gray-500">|</span></span><span><a class="text-color-gray-500" href="" rel="noreferrer" target="_blank">Your Privacy Choices</a></span></div></div></div></footer></div><script id="__NEXT_DATA__" type="application/json">{"props":{"pageProps":{"data":{"post":{"_id":"909e025e-2d86-46f9-a47c-8bf272429818","author":{"name":"Author","picture":null},"content":{"attachedResources":null,"collectionItems":null,"contentBody":[{"_key":"5d4a1358c67d","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"d60f57c24972","_type":"span","marks":["714459fbf161"],"text":"Plate tectonics"},{"_key":"0e782137bba2","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" is a scientific theory that explains how major landforms are created as a result of Earth’s subterranean movements. The theory, which solidified in the 1960s, transformed the earth sciences by explaining many phenomena, including mountain building events, "},{"_key":"ca9518202cbd","_type":"span","marks":["74ac89b25adf"],"text":"volcanoes"},{"_key":"d07180f703e0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" and "},{"_key":"aee7a4734033","_type":"span","marks":["f543dfc59f64"],"text":"earthquakes"},{"_key":"b740ce3881c4","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":".\n\nIn plate tectonics, Earth’s outermost layer, or "},{"_key":"563f2d59d7fc","_type":"span","marks":["1d1480bc1964"],"text":"lithosphere"},{"_key":"16c21fb27df7","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"—made up of the crust and upper mantle—is broken into large rocky plates. These plates lie on top of a partially "},{"_key":"89d518f4d1a9","_type":"span","marks":["f92b90976f5e"],"text":"molten"},{"_key":"693dd39ea8ea","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" layer of rock called the "},{"_key":"8c0a2ba458a6","_type":"span","marks":["752333a0ba47"],"text":"asthenosphere"},{"_key":"00b1be8b932d","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":". Due to the "},{"_key":"368812e8c37c","_type":"span","marks":["61a961b98c32"],"text":"convection"},{"_key":"44b6e99b5c12","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" of the asthenosphere and lithosphere, the plates move relative to each other at different rates, from two to 15 centimeters (one to six inches) per year. This "},{"_key":"09ae6aee5cd5","_type":"span","marks":["49f4913c2287"],"text":"interaction"},{"_key":"2a6aa40265e2","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" of "},{"_key":"65d0f1893029","_type":"span","marks":["97f5713cee91"],"text":"tectonic plates"},{"_key":"90fd5370cb43","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" is responsible for many different geological formations such as the Himalaya mountain range in Asia, the East African Rift and the San Andreas Fault in California, United States.\n\nThe idea that continents moved over time had been proposed before the 20th century. However, a German scientist named Alfred Wegener changed the scientific debate. Wegener published two articles about a concept called "},{"_key":"265f0cccaf40","_type":"span","marks":["ff6084a660ff"],"text":"continental drift"},{"_key":"0c639b51195d","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" in 1912. He suggested that 200 million years ago, a "},{"_key":"c3787dcfdeda","_type":"span","marks":["59d17b244cac"],"text":"supercontinent"},{"_key":"45d55da03332","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" he called Pangaea began to break into pieces, its parts moving away from one another. The continents we see today are fragments of that supercontinent. To support his theory, Wegener pointed to matching rock formations and similar fossils in Brazil and West Africa. In addition, South America and Africa looked like they could fit together like puzzle pieces.\n\nDespite being dismissed at first, the theory gained steam in the 1950s and 1960s as new data began to support the idea of continental drift. Maps of the ocean floor showed a massive undersea mountain range that almost circled the entire Earth. An American "},{"_key":"c8bbb64c3e05","_type":"span","marks":["d4a1b861c042"],"text":"geologist"},{"_key":"e72fd7bc0672","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" named Harry Hess proposed that these ridges were the result of molten rock rising from the asthenosphere. As it came to the surface, the rock cooled, making new crust and spreading the seafloor away from the ridge in a conveyer-belt motion. Millions of years later, the crust would disappear into ocean trenches at places called "},{"_key":"856cdc147803","_type":"span","marks":["c4d4320fe799"],"text":"subduction"},{"_key":"ba63c1a8d4ad","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":" zones and cycle back into Earth. Magnetic data from the ocean floor and the relatively young age of oceanic crust supported Hess’s hypothesis of "},{"_key":"c1da6aeadaa6","_type":"span","marks":["bdf55e40c5a9"],"text":"seafloor spreading"},{"_key":"7f9e902bd4cc","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":".\n\nThere was one nagging question with the plate tectonics theory: Most volcanoes are found above subduction zones, but some form far away from these plate boundaries. How could this be explained? This question was finally answered in 1963 by a Canadian geologist, John Tuzo Wilson. He proposed that volcanic island chains, like the Hawaiian Islands, are created by fixed “hot spots” in the mantle. At those places, magma forces its way upward through the moving plate of the sea floor. As the plate moves over the hot spot, one volcanic island after another is formed. Wilson’s explanation gave further support to plate tectonics. Today, the theory is almost universally accepted."}],"markDefs":[{"_key":"714459fbf161","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"0ae6ca6c-a48e-421c-ae07-32fa0297ae26","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"74ac89b25adf","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"efae901f-4142-4d58-bbff-84184c94d4bd","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"f543dfc59f64","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"60242ed0-d9a6-4d45-b972-733d9a9f3f77","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"1d1480bc1964","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"9a3e5d3e-c9e3-4747-a69e-971370b6dcf7","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"f92b90976f5e","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"261bf785-73a4-480c-9855-8a1cdc9d7840","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"752333a0ba47","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"4c7ed4e4-062c-46d4-9d84-70b3bb4d80f7","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"61a961b98c32","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"00219f2c-c83f-467a-843b-5b9868b74524","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"49f4913c2287","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"018d7a2c-307e-4781-b9c6-18aa8d7a0677","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"97f5713cee91","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"5b3587f3-1ee6-4379-900b-c49255734a41","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"ff6084a660ff","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"ff72a2b3-1c7d-4e90-8e92-47a070b01d97","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"59d17b244cac","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"f666ae1e-1831-4dab-b2fa-016833137922","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"d4a1b861c042","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"2445898a-47c7-4190-b551-68da9076aacd","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"c4d4320fe799","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"febdaff1-fbaa-45a9-bea6-9d0a25ae3b10","_type":"reference"}},{"_key":"bdf55e40c5a9","_type":"vocabularyItem","item":{"_ref":"f4d4e03d-0bf4-45d7-a3aa-62d9eb7e5556","_type":"reference"}}],"style":"normal"},{"_key":"4d605ea53356","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"4d605ea533560","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":""}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"contentBodyLabel":"Article","exploreMoreLinks":null,"fastFacts":null,"historicDates":null,"ideas":null,"instructionalLinks":null,"interactive":null,"questionAnswerList":null,"sponsors":null,"video":null,"vocabulary":[{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:39:04Z","_id":"4c7ed4e4-062c-46d4-9d84-70b3bb4d80f7","_rev":"coLQB43m6xeYDxgzOpLQqf","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:28:36Z","definition":[{"_key":"8787a13df065","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"8787a13df0650","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"layer in Earth's mantle between the lithosphere (above) and the upper mantle (below)."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"asthenosphere","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(as-THEH-nuh-sfeer)","word":"asthenosphere"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:39:20Z","_id":"ff72a2b3-1c7d-4e90-8e92-47a070b01d97","_rev":"ZRH6ZdBMeV906OhKdkZHJV","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:28:47Z","definition":[{"_key":"6e66d55fc3d2","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"6e66d55fc3d20","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"the movement of continents resulting from the motion of tectonic plates."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"continental-drift","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(kahn-tih-NEHN-tuhl DRIHFT)","word":"continental drift"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:39:20Z","_id":"00219f2c-c83f-467a-843b-5b9868b74524","_rev":"ZRH6ZdBMeV906OhKdkZHJV","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:28:47Z","definition":[{"_key":"c1d0e3496a2a","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"c1d0e3496a2a0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"transfer of heat by the movement of the heated parts of a liquid or gas."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"convection","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(kun-VEK-shun)","word":"convection"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:39:32Z","_id":"60242ed0-d9a6-4d45-b972-733d9a9f3f77","_rev":"CIhIiuJe6OyNVYaAjamJ44","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:28:57Z","definition":[{"_key":"ab86fe89f75c","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"ab86fe89f75c0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"the sudden shaking of Earth's crust caused by the release of energy along fault lines or from volcanic activity."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"earthquake","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(URTH-kwayk)","word":"earthquake"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:39:35Z","_id":"2445898a-47c7-4190-b551-68da9076aacd","_rev":"ZRH6ZdBMeV906OhKdkZY7R","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:28:58Z","definition":[{"_key":"c3e59de2709f","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"c3e59de2709f0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"person who studies the physical formations of the Earth."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"geologist","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(jee-AH-luh-jihst)","word":"geologist"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:39:48Z","_id":"018d7a2c-307e-4781-b9c6-18aa8d7a0677","_rev":"coLQB43m6xeYDxgzOpLnF3","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:29:09Z","definition":[{"_key":"b07dc8071386","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"b07dc80713860","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"relationship between two or more forces, objects, or organisms."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"interaction","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(ihn-tur-AK-shun)","word":"interaction"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:39:48Z","_id":"9a3e5d3e-c9e3-4747-a69e-971370b6dcf7","_rev":"coLQB43m6xeYDxgzOpLnG5","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:29:09Z","definition":[{"_key":"450343730a6b","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"450343730a6b0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"outer, solid portion of the Earth. Also called the geosphere."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"lithosphere","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(LIH-thuh-sfeer)","word":"lithosphere"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:40:02Z","_id":"261bf785-73a4-480c-9855-8a1cdc9d7840","_rev":"CIhIiuJe6OyNVYaAjamWnE","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:29:21Z","definition":[{"_key":"69f6653afcc7","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"69f6653afcc70","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"solid material turned to liquid by heat."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"molten","partOfSpeech":"adjective","phonetic":"(MOHL-tehn)","word":"molten"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:40:12Z","_id":"0ae6ca6c-a48e-421c-ae07-32fa0297ae26","_rev":"CIhIiuJe6OyNVYaAjamjqL","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:29:31Z","definition":[{"_key":"40f207e39ab5","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"40f207e39ab50","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"movement and interaction of the Earth's plates."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"plate-tectonics","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(PLAYT tehk-TAH-nihks)","word":"plate tectonics"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:40:28Z","_id":"f4d4e03d-0bf4-45d7-a3aa-62d9eb7e5556","_rev":"coLQB43m6xeYDxgzOpMqVV","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:29:42Z","definition":[{"_key":"77c8bbea3ce6","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"77c8bbea3ce60","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"rift in underwater mountain range where new oceanic crust is formed."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"seafloor-spreading","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(SEE-flohr SPREH-ding)","word":"seafloor spreading"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:40:31Z","_id":"febdaff1-fbaa-45a9-bea6-9d0a25ae3b10","_rev":"ZRH6ZdBMeV906OhKdkZp3R","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:29:43Z","definition":[{"_key":"86b386da7aaf","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"86b386da7aaf0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"process of one tectonic plate melting, sliding, or falling beneath another."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"subduction","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(sub-DUK-shun)","word":"subduction"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:40:31Z","_id":"f666ae1e-1831-4dab-b2fa-016833137922","_rev":"CIhIiuJe6OyNVYaAjamxDq","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:29:44Z","definition":[{"_key":"d89f7e0d6864","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"d89f7e0d68640","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"ancient, giant landmass that split apart to form all the continents we know today."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"supercontinent","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(SOO-pur-kahn-tih-nehnt)","word":"supercontinent"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:40:31Z","_id":"5b3587f3-1ee6-4379-900b-c49255734a41","_rev":"CIhIiuJe6OyNVYaAjamxDq","_type":"ngs.rl.vocabularyItem","_updatedAt":"2022-05-13T06:29:44Z","definition":[{"_key":"9e99f99dbf1f","_type":"block","children":[{"_key":"9e99f99dbf1f0","_type":"span","marks":[],"text":"massive slab of solid rock made up of Earth's lithosphere (crust and upper mantle). Also called lithospheric plate."}],"markDefs":[],"style":"normal"}],"displayName":"tectonic-plate","partOfSpeech":"noun","phonetic":"(tehk-TAHN-ihk PLAYT)","word":"tectonic plate"},{"_createdAt":"2022-05-09T10:40:46Z","_id":"efae901f-4142-4d58-bbff-84184c